1087646484375, 0.0242767333984375, 0.0229339599609375, 0.0303497314453125, -0.013153076171875, 0.0009918212890625, -0.00835418701171875, 0.018341064453125, -0.00555419921875, -0.0092620849609375, 0.045501708984375, -0.0252532958984375, 0.0189971923828125, 0.00928497314453125, 0.004322052001953125, 0.02716064453125, 0.01275634765625, 0.039398193359375, -0.0204620361328125, 0.03656005859375, 0.0039825439453125, 0.028228759765625, -0.0186920166015625, 0.01009368896484375, 0.0088043212890625, -0.006931304931640625, -0.0034198760986328125, -0.00254058837890625, -0.006694793701171875, 0.0040740966796875, 0.048492431640625, -0.006267547607421875, -0.01293182373046875, -0.02294921875, 0.0004405975341796875, -0.00969696044921875, -0.017181396484375, 0.0328369140625, 0.027435302734375, 0.0030517578125, -0.0188140869140625, -0.0194854736328125, -0.0096588134765625, 0.00742340087890625, -0.033050537109375, 0.0273590087890625, -0.040802001953125, -0.004306793212890625, -4.410743713378906e-06, 0.00045561790466308594, 0.02081298828125, 0.0085906982421875, -0.0092620849609375, -0.0140380859375], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Large-scale image generation has advanced through improvements in data scale, quality, rebalancing, and recaptioning. However, most existing pipelines optimize datasets for individual tasks in isolation, limiting the ability to leverage shared knowledge across related generative tasks. Our work introduces a fundamentally new approach: a capability-driven data infrastructure that systematically organizes and integrates supervision for multiple generative capabilities, enabling curriculum-based training and interoperability across tasks. Unlike prior work, which typically focuses on single-task curation or ad hoc multi-task extensions, our framework explicitly models task dependencies, integrates data engines with shared annotation layers, and schedules data exposure according to a principled curriculum.\n\nThe capability-driven data infrastructure consists of three interoperable data engines, each responsible for a distinct supervision type, and a curriculum scheduler that sequences data presentation. Concretely, the infrastructure operates as follows:\n\n1. **Text-Image Grounding Engine:** This engine constructs datasets by crawling public web sources and open repositories for image-caption pairs. Automated quality filters (e.g., CLIP-based relevance scoring, language model toxicity checks) are applied, followed by human review for a random 5% sample to calibrate thresholds. Caption experts\u2014captioning models fine-tuned with human feedback\u2014are used to standardize and enrich captions. The engine outputs a corpus of 440M high-quality image-caption pairs.\n\n2. **Inter-Image Transformation Engine:** This engine assembles editing pairs by generating synthetic edits (using automated tools for color, object, or style changes) and collecting real-world edits from open datasets. Each pair includes the original image, the edited image, and a standardized transformation instruction generated by caption experts. Consistency checks ensure that instructions accurately describe the transformation. The engine produces 120M editing pairs.\n\n3. **Image-Knowledge Association Engine:** This engine links images to structured knowledge entities by applying automated entity recognition (using pretrained vision-language models) and mapping to external knowledge bases (e.g., Wikidata). Manual validation is performed on a stratified sample to ensure mapping accuracy. The engine outputs 27M image-entity pairs.\n\n**Interoperability and Data Integration:** Outputs from the three engines are merged via a shared annotation schema. For example, an image-caption pair is linked to its knowledge entities and can serve as a base for generating editing pairs. Conflicts (e.g., inconsistent captions or entity mappings) are resolved by majority voting among caption experts and human annotators. Redundancies are detected by hashing image features and deduplicating at the dataset level.\n\n**Curriculum Learning Strategy:** The curriculum scheduler organizes data presentation in three stages: (1) T2I grounding, (2) editing, and (3) knowledge association. Stage transitions are determined by monitoring validation loss plateaus and task-specific performance thresholds (e.g., FID < 10 for T2I before introducing editing data). Task dependencies are established based on empirical ablation studies: models trained first on T2I grounding exhibit better transfer to editing and knowledge tasks. The rationale is that foundational grounding skills facilitate more complex transformations and associations.\n\n**Evaluation Methodology:** We train multimodal diffusion models from scratch at 3B and 6B parameters using the curated datasets. Training is conducted on 512 A100 GPUs for 14 days (3B) and 28 days (6B), with mixed-precision optimization and reproducible random seeds. Dataset splits are 90% train, 5% validation, 5% test, stratified by source and content. Baselines include state-of-the-art T2I and editing models (e.g., Stable Diffusion, Imagen Editor). Quantitative benchmarking on CPI-Bench measures FID, CLIP score, and editing accuracy; statistical significance is assessed via paired t-tests (p < 0.01). Human preference scores are collected from 50 expert raters, each evaluating 200 randomly sampled generations per model, with inter-rater agreement measured by Fleiss\u2019 kappa (>0.75).\n\n**Summary of Figures 2\u20134:** Figure 2 illustrates the data engine workflows and integration points. Figure 3 presents qualitative examples of T2I generations, edits, and knowledge associations, highlighting visual diversity and task transfer. Figure 4 compares model outputs to baselines, showing improved image quality and edit fidelity.\n\n**Bias Mitigation and Quality Control:** Automated filters remove images with detected sensitive content (using NSFW classifiers) and flag demographic imbalances (using attribute classifiers). Human annotators review flagged samples, and dataset composition is adjusted to ensure representation across genders, ages, and geographies. Metrics include the proportion of flagged content (<0.5%) and demographic parity scores (>0.9 across major groups). Effectiveness is validated by comparing model outputs for bias using the FairFace benchmark, with observed bias reduced by 30% relative to baselines.\n\n**Computational Resources and Reproducibility:** All experiments are conducted on a cluster of 512 A100 GPUs, with training times and hyperparameters logged for reproducibility. Code, data processing scripts, and model checkpoints will be released to facilitate fair comparison and replication.\n\nOur models achieve competitive or superior performance on CPI-Bench, with FID improvements of 5\u201310% over prior methods and strong human preference scores for image quality and edit fidelity. Qualitative results demonstrate broad visual coverage, versatile rendering, and effective transfer between generative tasks, as summarized above."}}